WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:08.742
These iconic monuments are
just shouting, screaming at us.

00:00:08.808 --> 00:00:12.278
Power, dominance, control.

00:00:12.345 --> 00:00:13.847
I feel about this.

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:16.883
I feel so insignificant.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:21.421
Together these pyramids and
the Sphinx and the temples

00:00:21.488 --> 00:00:23.456
create a landscape of power.

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:31.331
NARRATOR: The huge sculpture
protects the necropolis.

00:00:31.398 --> 00:00:33.633
A giant symbol of
supremacy believed

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:37.303
by many to have the face
of Khufu's son, Khafre,

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.940
owner of Giza's second
largest pyramid.

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:46.479
Despite many visits here,
John has never been up close.

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:49.048
Today he's been
granted special access

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:53.319
to explore its enclosure.

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:55.488
JOHN: It's absolutely inspiring.

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:59.292
I mean, it's just jaw-dropping.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:05.165
I've waited 50 years to
be here, and now I'm here.

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:07.734
It's just wow.

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:11.004
NARRATOR: 66 feet
high, the Sphinx gazes

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:13.973
east towards the rising sun.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.043
JOHN: I know what it's like
to work with the living rock

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:19.846
and how to carve it.

00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:23.450
This would have been a
monumental challenge.

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:29.289
They only had copper
chisels, wooden mallets.

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:31.057
It would have been
a harsh environment.

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:33.693
This dust would have
been everywhere.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:37.597
And yet the workmen, the
craftsmen, the masons,

00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:41.601
they were all willing
participants, loyal to pharaoh.

00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:47.240
NARRATOR: It would have taken
thousands of people decades

00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:51.478
to construct the epic
monuments of Giza,

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:53.313
but there's no
evidence the pharaohs

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:56.316
enslaved people to build them.

00:01:56.382 --> 00:02:00.487
They didn't draw their power
over the people from force.

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:03.857
John believes what stood
these kings apart was

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:05.425
that they inspired devotion.

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:13.533
The sheer volume of stone
has gone into building this,

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:19.038
and each individual block
represents the loyalty

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:23.076
that they had for Pharaoh.

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:24.978
NARRATOR: The huge
monuments of Giza

00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:28.147
represent the peak
of the pyramid age.

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:30.817
So why was nothing built
on this scale again?

00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:38.258
At the height of Khufu's
reign, reliable seasonal rains

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:41.628
fed the crops that ensured
Egypt's prosperity.

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:47.400
For three months
every year, the Nile

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:51.004
flooded and inundated
the farmland, so farmers

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.338
couldn't work the fields.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:59.045
The farmers were free to help
build the King's enormous tomb.

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:04.317
They believed he kept the gods
content and the country fed.

00:03:04.384 --> 00:03:07.453
But soon after Khufu
completed his pyramid,

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.589
Egypt began to suffer drought.

00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:18.698
As crop yields crashed,
so did the taxes coming

00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:22.602
into the state's treasuries,
and despite the free labor

00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:26.573
the Giza monuments
almost bankrupted Egypt.

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:29.409
The people's loyalty
began to falter.

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:33.212
The King's tombs that
followed got smaller

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:37.050
while Khufu's Great Pyramid
remained, dominating

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:40.219
the Nile's West Bank.

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:44.757
JOHN: The sheer
power that he held

00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:47.293
is absolutely unbelievable.

00:03:49.696 --> 00:03:52.265
NARRATOR: It was a power
his struggling successors

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:54.367
were desperate to replicate.

00:03:57.437 --> 00:04:01.541
At Abu Ghurab
Italian archeologist

00:04:01.608 --> 00:04:04.544
Massimiliano Nuzzola
wants to know

00:04:04.611 --> 00:04:08.314
how the kings of the pyramid age
held onto power as their wealth

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:11.317
and status declined.

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:14.854
He has spent his entire career
trying to understand a very

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:18.625
different monumental structure.

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.860
An enigma whose
mysteries captured

00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:24.063
his imagination in his
very first student course

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:25.298
in Egyptology.

00:04:27.734 --> 00:04:30.336
This enormous scatter
of ancient rubble

00:04:30.403 --> 00:04:34.374
was once a sun temple
dedicated to the most powerful

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.908
god of the pyramid age.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.779
MASSIMILLIANO NUZZOLA:
Each king wanted a pyramid

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:42.215
for achieving his
resurrection but this

00:04:42.281 --> 00:04:44.250
was not enough for the
five dynastic kings.

00:04:44.317 --> 00:04:46.586
They wanted something more.

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:50.990
The king built this place
to turn himself into a god.

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:53.259
The sun god.

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:55.828
NARRATOR: The Pharaoh
Nyuserre didn't just

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:59.332
want to become divine in death.

00:04:59.399 --> 00:05:01.234
He built this temple
because he wanted

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:04.170
to be worshipped as a god
while he was still alive.

00:05:07.073 --> 00:05:10.977
Rising from the desert
was an enormous obelisk,

00:05:11.044 --> 00:05:14.313
the centerpiece of the temple.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.350
It was not a traditional
slender stone needle,

00:05:17.417 --> 00:05:20.987
but shaped more like a pyramid.

00:05:21.054 --> 00:05:23.589
Monumental walls
enclosed it creating

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:28.461
a courtyard in which people
could come to worship the sun.

00:05:28.528 --> 00:05:31.364
The temple aligned
perfectly on an east west

00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:35.001
axis with the path of the sun.

00:05:35.068 --> 00:05:37.370
So on the summer
solstice every year,

00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:40.006
the sun rose through
the entrance,

00:05:40.073 --> 00:05:42.408
traveled directly
over the obelisk,

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:45.678
and set at the Western end.

00:05:45.745 --> 00:05:48.915
This alignment is identical
to that of the pyramids.

00:05:53.853 --> 00:05:57.657
Records suggest that six of
the pharaohs who followed Khufu

00:05:57.724 --> 00:06:01.260
decided to build a sun temple
as well as their pyramid

00:06:01.327 --> 00:06:06.966
to underline their divine
status to the people of Egypt.

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:09.135
But almost all of
these temples are lost.

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:11.070
MASSIMILLIANO NUZZOLA:
We know that there were

00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:13.206
six sun temples, and
we actually so far

00:06:13.272 --> 00:06:14.774
have discovered only two.

00:06:14.841 --> 00:06:18.611
NARRATOR: Max hopes excavating
this sun temple will shed light

00:06:18.678 --> 00:06:21.781
on these kings
power, and perhaps

00:06:21.848 --> 00:06:25.351
help him crack one of the
biggest mysteries in Egyptology

00:06:25.418 --> 00:06:29.555
and find the other
missing sun temples.

00:06:29.622 --> 00:06:33.659
The problem is, Max is
not the first to decode

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:36.429
this structure's secrets.

00:06:36.496 --> 00:06:41.100
Early Egyptologists excavated
here more than 100 years ago,

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:43.669
and left a mess of
archaeological confusion

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:46.539
in their wake.

00:06:46.606 --> 00:06:49.175
Well, this find is
amazing because it's

00:06:49.242 --> 00:06:52.979
a cover of matches left
by the archaeologists

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:54.614
during their work.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.316
NARRATOR: Along with their
discarded matchboxes,

00:06:57.383 --> 00:07:02.421
early 20th century excavators
left 4,500 year old pottery

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:04.624
scattered across the site.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.726
As you can see here,
this area of the temple

00:07:06.793 --> 00:07:08.294
is full of pot shells.

00:07:08.361 --> 00:07:11.297
But unfortunately this material
is completely useless for us

00:07:11.364 --> 00:07:13.766
because it's out of
a secure context.

00:07:13.833 --> 00:07:16.769
Cannot tell us anything
about the life of the temple.

00:07:16.836 --> 00:07:20.273
NARRATOR: If Max can find
undisturbed artifacts,

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:24.877
they might reveal clues the
first archeologists missed.

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:27.046
Even the smallest
of finds could be

00:07:27.113 --> 00:07:34.086
crucial in the hunt for the lost
sun temples of the pyramid age.

00:07:34.153 --> 00:07:37.123
[music playing]

